Title:
Don’t Let Abliteration Abliterate Your Bug Hunting: Discovering Verdict Bias in Uncensored Models

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post evaluates “abliterated” (uncensored via weight-editing) open-weight LLMs for vulnerability research and shows they can introduce strong “verdict bias”: a tendency to confirm bugs despite contradictory evidence.  
- Using a scan→triage pipeline over FreeBSD kernel RPC code (including a known CVE), the author finds abliterated variants generate ~3–4× more “VALID” findings and may confirm obvious false positives that base models correctly reject.  
- In the most aggressive build tested, the model produced many confident false positives and failed to surface the real CVE at all, indicating the issue is not just extra noise but potentially missed true positives due to miscalibrated judgment and early stopping.  
- The post argues abliteration removes more than refusal behavior; it appears to also ablate “reluctance to commit under uncertainty,” impacting triage decisions rather than raw knowledge.  
- It provides simple non-code probes (facts vs flattery/optimism) and a reproducible test harness to measure yes-bias per specific model build/quant before trusting it in a vuln pipeline.  
- Useful for red teamers, vulnerability researchers, and anyone using local LLMs for code auditing/RE who needs calibrated, skeptical validation rather than maximal “findings.”

Technical Focus:
- LLM abliteration / refusal-direction weight editing (difference-in-means, residual stream direction removal)
- Vulnerability scanning and triage pipelines (candidate generation vs validation)
- False positive inflation and calibration/decision-threshold shifts (“verdict bias”)
- Empirical evaluation on FreeBSD kernel RPC code and CVE surfacing behavior
- Prompting for skeptical review and measuring model disposition under uncertainty

Use Cases:
- Benchmarking base vs “uncensored” local models for vulnerability research quality (FP rate, miss rate)
- Designing acceptance tests to detect yes-bias before integrating a model into scan/triage workflows
- Selecting/validating specific GGUF builds and quantizations for agentic RE/code audit pipelines
- Building guardrails around LLM-assisted triage (e.g., adversarial “known-false” test cases like bounded-buffer examples)

Keywords:
abliteration, refusal direction, weight editing, difference-in-means, residual stream, uncensored LLM, verdict bias, calibration, false positives, vulnerability triage, code auditing, FreeBSD kernel, RPC, RPCSEC_GSS, CVE-2026-4747, XDR, xdrmem_create, xdr_opaque_auth, GGUF, LM Studio, nano-analyzer, local models, Gemma, Qwen, prompt engineering